   "html": "<h2>0.1 Welcome to the Course</h2>\n<p>Imagine you get an SMS: “Dear customer, your electricity connection will be cut tonight at 9:30 PM. Call our officer immediately.” Your heart beats a little faster. You don’t want to sit in the dark, so you call. Five minutes later, the “officer” has you installing a “bill payment app” on your phone — and your bank account is empty.</p>\n<p>That is phishing. No hacking of servers, no complex code. Just one message designed to make a normal person act in a hurry.</p>\n<p>This course will teach you how these tricks work, how to spot them, and what to do if you (or someone around you) falls for one. You don’t need any technical background — if you use a phone, email, or WhatsApp, this course is for you.</p>\n<h2>0.2 Who Is This Course For?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>College students:</strong> the favourite target of fake internship offers, scholarship scams, and “part-time job, earn ₹5,000 per day” messages.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Employees in any organisation:</strong> you receive emails that look like they come from your boss, HR, or a vendor. One wrong click can put the whole company at risk.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>IT and security staff:</strong> you will use this course to build awareness in your teams and handle phishing reports with confidence.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>0.3 Why Phishing Still Works in 2026</h2>\n<p>Companies spend crores on firewalls, antivirus, and security tools. Yet phishing remains the number one way attackers get in. Why?</p>\n<p><strong>Because it attacks people, not machines.</strong> Think of it like this: you can install the strongest lock on your door, but if someone in a courier uniform knocks and politely asks you to open it, the lock doesn’t matter. Phishing is that fake courier.</p>\n<p><strong>And it is getting harder to spot.</strong> In the past, phishing emails were full of spelling mistakes and strange English. Today, attackers use AI tools to write perfect, personalised messages, and can even clone a person’s voice from a short video clip. The old advice of “look for bad grammar” is no longer enough.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout example\"><h4>Real-Life Example: The Twitter Hack (2020)</h4>\n<p>In July 2020, the Twitter accounts of Barack Obama, Elon Musk, Bill Gates, Apple, and many others suddenly posted: “Send me Bitcoin and I’ll send back double.”</p>\n<p>How did the attackers get in? Not by breaking Twitter’s code. They phoned Twitter employees pretending to be from the company’s internal IT team, and convinced them to enter their login details on a fake website. With those credentials, they reached Twitter’s internal admin tools.</p>\n<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> One of the world’s biggest tech companies was breached with a phone call. If it can happen to them, it can happen to anyone.</p></aside>\n<h2>0.4 What You Will Learn</h2>\n<p>By the end of this course, you will be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Explain what phishing is and how it has evolved, including AI-powered attacks.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recognise different types of phishing — email, SMS, calls, QR codes, and social media.</p></li>\n<li><p>Understand the psychology attackers use to manipulate people.</p></li>\n<li><p>Spot red flags in emails, links, and messages, and verify them using free tools.</p></li>\n<li><p>Take practical precautions to protect yourself and your organisation.</p></li>\n<li><p>Respond correctly if you click a phishing link, and report it to the right authorities.</p></li>\n<li><p>Understand your responsibilities under your organisation’s Acceptable Use Policy and Indian law.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>0.5 Course Structure</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Module</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Topic</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What You Get</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Course Introduction</td>\n<td>Why this matters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>What is Phishing</td>\n<td>Basic concepts and terms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Evolution of Phishing</td>\n<td>From 1990s scams to AI deepfakes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Types of Phishing</td>\n<td>Email, SMS, calls, QR, social media</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Impact and Risk</td>\n<td>Cost to individuals and companies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Social Engineering Techniques</td>\n<td>The psychology behind attacks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Anatomy of a Phishing Attack</td>\n<td>How an attack is built, step by step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>How to Identify Phishing</td>\n<td>Red flags + hands-on tools</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Common Phishing Attacks</td>\n<td>Scams you will actually see</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Precautions to Stay Safe</td>\n<td>Personal and organisational defences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Incident Response</td>\n<td>What to do if you clicked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Legal Framework (India)</td>\n<td>IT Act and DPDP Act basics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Acceptable Use Policy</td>\n<td>Your responsibilities at work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Real-Life Case Studies</td>\n<td>Learning from real attacks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Final Assessment &amp; Certification</td>\n<td>Quiz, practical, certificate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>0.6 How to Use This Course</h2>\n<p>Throughout the modules, look out for these boxes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>📌 Real-Life Example:</strong> true stories of phishing attacks and what we can learn from them.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>💡 Quick Tip:</strong> simple habits you can start using today.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>✏️ Try It Yourself:</strong> short activities to practise what you learned.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>❓ Quick Check:</strong> a few questions at the end of each module to test your understanding.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Go through the modules in order — each one builds on the previous. Complete the Final Assessment in Module 14 to earn your CPPS certificate.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout tip\"><h4>Quick Tip: Start Today</h4>\n<p>Before moving to Module 1, remember one golden rule that this entire course is built on:</p>\n<p><strong>Pause. Verify. Then act.</strong></p>\n<p>Any message that makes you feel rushed, scared, or excited is a reason to slow down — not speed up.</p></aside>",
