   "title": "What is Phishing",
   "time": "30 minutes",
   "html": "<h2>1.1 The Simple Definition</h2>\n<p><strong>Phishing is a trick where an attacker pretends to be someone you trust, so that you give away something valuable</strong> — your password, OTP, bank details, personal data, or money — or do something harmful, like installing a malicious app.</p>\n<p>The word comes from “fishing”. The attacker throws out bait (a message), waits for someone to bite (click, reply, or call), and then reels them in. The “ph” spelling comes from early hacker culture, where “phreaking” meant hacking telephone systems.</p>\n<h2>1.2 The Three Ingredients of Every Phishing Attack</h2>\n<p>Almost every phishing attack, no matter how clever, has these three parts:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Ingredient</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What It Means</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1. A fake identity</strong></td>\n<td>The attacker pretends to be a trusted person or organisation.</td>\n<td>“SBI Customer Care”, “HR Team”, “Your Principal”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2. A hook (emotion)</strong></td>\n<td>It creates urgency, fear, greed, or curiosity so you don’t think.</td>\n<td>“Account blocked in 24 hours”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3. An action</strong></td>\n<td>It asks you to do something: click, log in, pay, call, share, install.</td>\n<td>“Update your KYC here: bit.ly/xyz”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout example\"><h4>Real-Life Example: The Classic KYC SMS</h4>\n<p>“Dear Customer, your bank account will be suspended today. Please update your PAN/KYC immediately: http://sbi-kyc-update.in”</p>\n<p><strong>Fake identity:</strong> looks like it’s from your bank. <strong>Hook:</strong> “suspended today” creates panic. <strong>Action:</strong> click the link and enter your details.</p>\n<p>The link leads to a website that looks exactly like the bank’s login page. Whatever you type — username, password, OTP — goes straight to the attacker.</p></aside>\n<h2>1.3 Phishing vs Spam vs Scam</h2>\n<p>People often mix these words up. Here’s the difference:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What It Is</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Goal</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spam</strong></td>\n<td>Unwanted bulk messages</td>\n<td>Mostly advertising (annoying, not always harmful)</td>\n<td>“50% off on shoes today!”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scam</strong></td>\n<td>Any trick to cheat you of money</td>\n<td>Money, by any method (online or offline)</td>\n<td>Fake lottery, fake investment scheme</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phishing</strong></td>\n<td>A scam using impersonation through digital channels</td>\n<td>Credentials, data, money, or device access</td>\n<td>Fake bank email asking you to log in</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In short: <strong>all phishing is a scam, but not every scam is phishing. And most spam is just annoying, not dangerous.</strong></p>\n<h2>1.4 Key Terms You Should Know</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Simple Meaning</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lure / Bait</strong></td>\n<td>The message designed to attract your attention.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spoofing</strong></td>\n<td>Faking the sender’s name, email, phone number, or website so it looks genuine.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Credential harvesting</strong></td>\n<td>Collecting usernames and passwords through fake login pages.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Malicious link</strong></td>\n<td>A link that takes you to a fake site or downloads harmful software.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Malicious attachment</strong></td>\n<td>A file (PDF, Word, Excel, ZIP, APK) that infects your device when opened.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Malware</strong></td>\n<td>Harmful software, e.g. viruses, spyware, ransomware, remote access apps.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Payload</strong></td>\n<td>What the attack actually delivers: a fake page, malware, or a payment request.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lookalike domain</strong></td>\n<td>A fake website address that closely resembles a real one, e.g. amaz0n.in or paytm-support.co.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>OTP</strong></td>\n<td>One-Time Password. Never share it with anyone — not even “bank officials”.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>1.5 What Do Attackers Want?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Your login credentials:</strong> email, bank, social media, company accounts.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Your money:</strong> direct transfers, UPI payments, gift cards, crypto.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Your personal data:</strong> Aadhaar, PAN, date of birth, address — used for identity theft or loan fraud.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Access to your device:</strong> by making you install a remote access app (like AnyDesk misused) or a fake APK.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Access to your organisation:</strong> your work account is a doorway to company data, systems, and other employees.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout tip\"><h4>Quick Tip: The OTP Rule</h4>\n<p>No genuine bank, company, government office, or delivery service will ever ask you for your OTP, PIN, or password. If someone asks, it is a scam. Every single time.</p></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout try\"><h4>Try It Yourself: Check Your Inbox</h4>\n<p>Open your SMS inbox and spam email folder. Find two messages that you now think could be phishing. For each, identify the three ingredients: the fake identity, the hook, and the action.</p></aside>",
