   "title": "Evolution of Phishing",
   "time": "35 minutes",
   "html": "<p>Phishing is not new. It has changed its clothes many times over 30 years, but the core trick — pretend to be trusted, create urgency, get an action — has stayed the same. Understanding its journey helps you predict where it’s going next.</p>\n<h2>2.1 The Timeline</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Era</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What Happened</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Typical Attack</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mid-1990s</strong></td>\n<td>The term “phishing” appears (around 1995–96) among hackers targeting AOL users.</td>\n<td>Fake AOL staff message: “Please verify your password.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Early 2000s</strong></td>\n<td>Online banking and e-commerce grow. Mass phishing emails target eBay, PayPal, and bank customers.</td>\n<td>“Your PayPal account is limited. Click to restore.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Late 2000s–2010s</strong></td>\n<td>Attackers stop spraying everyone and start targeting specific people and companies (spear phishing).</td>\n<td>Personalised email to a finance employee with a malicious attachment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2010s</strong></td>\n<td>Smartphones arrive. SMS (smishing) and phone call (vishing) attacks explode. Business Email Compromise (BEC) grows.</td>\n<td>“CEO” emails finance: “Urgent wire transfer, keep it confidential.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2020–2023</strong></td>\n<td>Pandemic and remote work. Fake Covid, WFH, and Microsoft 365 login pages. QR codes and MFA fatigue attacks rise.</td>\n<td>Flood of MFA pop-ups until the victim taps “Approve”.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2023–today</strong></td>\n<td>Generative AI and deepfakes. Perfect grammar, cloned voices, fake video calls.</td>\n<td>Video call with a deepfaked CFO ordering payments.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>2.2 Key Turning Points</h2>\n<h2>From mass to targeted</h2>\n<p>Early phishing was like throwing a net into the sea: send a million emails, and even if 0.1% fall for it, that’s a thousand victims. Modern attackers often prefer spear fishing — researching one person on LinkedIn or Instagram and crafting a message just for them.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout example\"><h4>Real-Life Example: RSA Security (2011)</h4>\n<p>Attackers sent emails titled “2011 Recruitment Plan” to small groups of employees at RSA, a major security company. The attached Excel file contained hidden malicious code. One employee retrieved the email from the junk folder and opened it.</p>\n<p>The attackers got inside and stole data related to RSA’s SecurID authentication tokens — used by thousands of organisations, including defence contractors.</p>\n<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Even a security company was breached with one well-targeted email.</p></aside>\n<h2>From email to every channel</h2>\n<p>Once people learned to be careful with email, attackers moved to SMS, WhatsApp, phone calls, social media DMs, and even QR codes pasted on posters. Wherever people communicate, phishing follows.</p>\n<h2>From breaking passwords to tiring people out</h2>\n<p>When companies adopted Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA), attackers adapted. In “MFA fatigue” attacks, they send dozens of login approval requests until a frustrated user taps “Approve” just to make it stop.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout example\"><h4>Real-Life Example: Uber (2022)</h4>\n<p>An attacker obtained an Uber contractor’s password, then repeatedly sent MFA approval requests to their phone. They then contacted the contractor on WhatsApp pretending to be Uber IT support, saying the requests would stop if they approved one. The contractor approved — and the attacker gained access to Uber’s internal systems.</p></aside>\n<h2>2.3 The AI Era: Phishing 3.0</h2>\n<p>Generative AI has changed phishing in three big ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Perfect language:</strong> AI writes fluent, professional emails in any language — English, Hindi, Tamil. Spelling mistakes are no longer a reliable red flag.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Mass personalisation:</strong> AI can scan your public profile and write a unique message referencing your college, job, or recent post — for thousands of people at once.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Deepfake voice and video:</strong> A few seconds of someone’s voice from a YouTube video or Instagram reel can be enough to clone it. Attackers can now call you as your “boss” or your “relative”.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout example\"><h4>Real-Life Example: The Deepfake CEO Voice (2019) and Arup Video Call (2024)</h4>\n<p>In 2019, the CEO of a UK energy company transferred about €220,000 after a phone call from what sounded exactly like his boss at the German parent company. It was an AI-cloned voice.</p>\n<p>In 2024, an employee at engineering firm Arup’s Hong Kong office joined a video call with what appeared to be the company’s CFO and several colleagues. Everyone on the call except the employee was a deepfake. The employee made transfers worth around US$25 million.</p>\n<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> “I saw them on video” or “I heard their voice” is no longer proof of identity.</p></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tip\"><h4>Quick Tip: Set a Family Code Word</h4>\n<p>Agree on a secret code word with your family. If someone calls claiming to be a relative in trouble and asking for money urgently, ask for the code word. A voice clone won’t know it.</p></aside>",
