   "n": 3,
   "title": "Types of Phishing",
   "time": "45 minutes",
   "html": "<p>Phishing comes in many forms. Some are named after the channel (SMS, voice, QR), and some after the target (a specific person, a senior executive). Knowing the names helps you recognise them and report them correctly.</p>\n<h2>3.1 Types by Target</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Type</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Who Is Targeted</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mass / Bulk Phishing</strong></td>\n<td>Everyone. Same message to thousands.</td>\n<td>“Your Netflix payment failed. Update your card.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spear Phishing</strong></td>\n<td>A specific person or small group, after research.</td>\n<td>“Hi Priya, following up on your application for the Data Analyst internship at Infosys…”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Whaling</strong></td>\n<td>Senior leaders: CEOs, CFOs, directors (the “big fish”).</td>\n<td>Fake legal notice or tax document sent to the CFO.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Business Email Compromise (BEC)</strong></td>\n<td>Finance, HR, and accounts teams.</td>\n<td>“CEO” asks accounts to urgently transfer money to a new vendor account.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>3.2 Types by Channel</h2>\n<h2>Email Phishing</h2>\n<p>The most common form. Fake emails that look like they come from banks, companies, colleges, or colleagues, containing malicious links or attachments.</p>\n<h2>Clone Phishing</h2>\n<p>The attacker copies a genuine email you received earlier (like a delivery notification or shared document) and resends it with the link or attachment swapped for a malicious one. Because it looks familiar, you trust it.</p>\n<h2>Smishing (SMS Phishing)</h2>\n<p>Phishing through SMS and messaging apps like WhatsApp and Telegram. Very common in India because almost everyone uses UPI and mobile banking.</p>\n<p>Example: “Your parcel is held at our warehouse due to incomplete address. Update here: indiapost-track.co”</p>\n<h2>Vishing (Voice Phishing)</h2>\n<p>Phishing over phone calls. The caller pretends to be from your bank, telecom company, police, customs, or tech support. Often combined with an SMS first.</p>\n<p>Example: “Main Mumbai Cyber Crime se bol raha hoon. Aapke Aadhaar se ek illegal parcel book hua hai.”</p>\n<h2>Quishing (QR Code Phishing)</h2>\n<p>Malicious QR codes that lead to fake websites or trigger payments. Attackers paste fake QR stickers over real ones at parking meters, shops, or on posters, or send QR codes in emails to escape link scanners.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\"><h4>Remember: UPI QR Codes</h4>\n<p>You only need to scan a QR code to <strong>pay</strong> money, never to <strong>receive</strong> it. If someone sends you a QR code saying “scan this to receive your refund” — it’s a scam.</p></aside>\n<h2>Social Media Phishing</h2>\n<p>Fake profiles, fake brand pages, fake customer support accounts, and hacked friends’ accounts sending messages like “Is this you in this video?” with a link. “Angler phishing” is when a fake support account replies to your public complaint about a company and asks you to DM your details.</p>\n<h2>Search Engine Phishing and Fake Customer Care</h2>\n<p>Attackers create fake websites or list fake customer care numbers that show up in search results or ads. You search “XYZ bank customer care number”, call the first number, and reach a scammer.</p>\n<h2>Pop-up and Tech Support Scams</h2>\n<p>A pop-up on your screen says “Your computer is infected! Call Microsoft Support at 1800-XXX.” The “support agent” asks you to install remote access software and pay for fake repairs.</p>\n<h2>Pharming</h2>\n<p>A more technical attack where you are redirected to a fake website even when you type the correct address, by tampering with DNS settings or your router. Less common for individuals, but it shows why you should watch for security warnings in your browser.</p>\n<h2>3.3 Quick Reference Table</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Type</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Channel</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Key Red Flag</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Email phishing</td>\n<td>Email</td>\n<td>Mismatched sender address or link</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spear phishing</td>\n<td>Email / DM</td>\n<td>Personal details used to build trust</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whaling</td>\n<td>Email</td>\n<td>Targets executives with legal/financial themes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BEC</td>\n<td>Email</td>\n<td>Urgent payment or bank-change request</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clone phishing</td>\n<td>Email</td>\n<td>Familiar email re-sent with a “new” link</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smishing</td>\n<td>SMS / WhatsApp</td>\n<td>Short links, urgency, unknown number</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vishing</td>\n<td>Phone call</td>\n<td>Caller asks for OTP, PIN, or app install</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quishing</td>\n<td>QR code</td>\n<td>QR from unknown source or “scan to receive”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Social media</td>\n<td>DMs / fake pages</td>\n<td>Unverified accounts, “support” DMing you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fake customer care</td>\n<td>Search results / ads</td>\n<td>Number not from the official website</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout try\"><h4>Try It Yourself: Name That Type</h4>\n<p>Classify each: (a) A call from “Airtel” asking you to install an app to fix your SIM. (b) An email to your company’s CFO about a “pending court case”. (c) A QR code on a WhatsApp message offering a cashback.</p>\n<p>Answers: (a) Vishing, (b) Whaling, (c) Quishing / smishing.</p></aside>",
