   "n": 4,
   "title": "Impact and Risk",
   "time": "30 minutes",
   "html": "<p>“It’s just one click” is how most disasters begin. In this module, we look at what phishing actually costs — for individuals and for organisations.</p>\n<h2>4.1 Impact on Individuals</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Impact</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What It Looks Like</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Financial loss</strong></td>\n<td>Money drained from bank accounts, UPI, credit cards, or wallets. Often unrecoverable if not reported quickly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Account takeover</strong></td>\n<td>Your email, Instagram, or WhatsApp is hijacked and used to scam your friends and family.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Identity theft</strong></td>\n<td>Your Aadhaar, PAN, and photos are used to take loans, open accounts, or register SIM cards in your name.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Privacy loss and blackmail</strong></td>\n<td>Private photos, chats, or documents stolen and used for extortion.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Emotional stress</strong></td>\n<td>Shame, anxiety, and self-blame. Many victims don’t report because they feel embarrassed.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\"><h4>Remember: Victims Are Not Stupid</h4>\n<p>Phishing is designed by professionals to fool smart people. Doctors, engineers, IT staff, and business leaders fall for it every day. The shame belongs to the criminal, not the victim. Reporting quickly matters more than anything else.</p></aside>\n<h2>4.2 Impact on Organisations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Direct financial loss:</strong> fraudulent payments, especially through BEC.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Data breach:</strong> customer, employee, and business data stolen and leaked or sold.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Ransomware:</strong> many ransomware attacks begin with a phishing email. Systems get locked until a ransom is paid.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Downtime:</strong> operations stop, hospitals delay treatment, factories halt production.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Legal and regulatory penalties:</strong> under India’s DPDP Act 2023, failing to protect personal data can attract heavy penalties (covered in Module 11).</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Reputation damage:</strong> customers and partners lose trust, which is often costlier than the attack itself.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout example\"><h4>Real-Life Example: Google and Facebook Lose Over $100 Million (2013–2015)</h4>\n<p>A Lithuanian man, Evaldas Rimasauskas, set up a fake company with the same name as Quanta Computer, a real Taiwanese hardware supplier that worked with Google and Facebook.</p>\n<p>He sent fake invoices and contracts to their finance teams over two years. The employees paid, believing they were paying a genuine vendor. Total losses exceeded $100 million. He was later arrested, pleaded guilty, and was sentenced to five years in prison.</p>\n<p><strong>Lesson:</strong> Even the biggest tech companies can be fooled by simple fake invoices if payment verification processes are weak.</p></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout example\"><h4>Real-Life Example: Ubiquiti Networks (2015)</h4>\n<p>Networking company Ubiquiti disclosed a loss of about $46.7 million after attackers impersonated company executives and sent fraudulent payment requests to its finance department.</p></aside>\n<h2>4.3 Understanding Risk</h2>\n<p>In security, <strong>Risk = Likelihood × Impact</strong>. Phishing is dangerous because both are high:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Likelihood is high:</strong> phishing messages are cheap to send and arrive every single day.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Impact is high:</strong> one successful click can give an attacker access to money, data, or an entire network.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Why one employee matters</h2>\n<p>In an organisation, you are connected to everything: shared drives, email lists, finance systems, customer data. An attacker who gets into your account can send phishing emails <strong>from your real address</strong> to your colleagues, who will trust them because they know you. This is how one click turns into a company-wide breach.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout tip\"><h4>Quick Tip: Think Like a Chain</h4>\n<p>A security chain is only as strong as its weakest link. Your alertness protects not just you, but everyone connected to you.</p></aside>",
