   "n": 5,
   "title": "Social Engineering Techniques",
   "time": "45 minutes",
   "html": "<h2>5.1 What is Social Engineering?</h2>\n<p><strong>Social engineering is the art of manipulating people into doing things they normally wouldn’t</strong> — sharing secrets, opening doors, or sending money. Phishing is the digital form of social engineering. Instead of hacking a computer, the attacker “hacks” the human mind.</p>\n<p>Think of a magician. The trick works not because the magic is real, but because the magician controls where you look. Social engineers do the same thing with your emotions.</p>\n<h2>5.2 The Psychology: Emotional Triggers</h2>\n<p>Attackers push the same few emotional buttons again and again. Once you know them, you’ll start noticing them everywhere.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Trigger</strong></th>\n<th><strong>How It Works</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example Line</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Urgency</strong></td>\n<td>No time to think = no time to verify.</td>\n<td>“Respond within 30 minutes or your account will be blocked.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fear</strong></td>\n<td>Panic shuts down logical thinking.</td>\n<td>“A case has been filed against you. You will be arrested.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Authority</strong></td>\n<td>We obey bosses, police, government, and banks.</td>\n<td>“This is the CEO. I need this done now.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Greed / Reward</strong></td>\n<td>Free money feels too good to question.</td>\n<td>“You’ve won ₹25 lakh in the KBC lottery!”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Curiosity</strong></td>\n<td>We want to know what’s behind the link.</td>\n<td>“Is this you in this video? 😱”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Helpfulness</strong></td>\n<td>Most people like to help others.</td>\n<td>“I’m new in IT, can you help me test your login?”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Social proof</strong></td>\n<td>If others are doing it, it must be safe.</td>\n<td>“5,000 students have already registered for this internship.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scarcity</strong></td>\n<td>Limited offers make us act fast.</td>\n<td>“Only 3 seats left! Pay registration fee now.”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout tip\"><h4>Quick Tip: Name the Emotion</h4>\n<p>When a message makes you feel a strong emotion — panic, excitement, fear — say it in your head: “This message is trying to make me feel scared.” Just naming the emotion gives your logical brain a chance to catch up.</p></aside>\n<h2>5.3 Common Social Engineering Techniques</h2>\n<h2>Pretexting</h2>\n<p>The attacker creates a believable story (a “pretext”) to get information. Example: calling an employee pretending to be from the IT helpdesk doing a “routine security audit” and asking for their login details.</p>\n<h2>Impersonation</h2>\n<p>Pretending to be someone trusted: a boss, a bank officer, a police officer, a delivery agent, a relative, or even a college professor.</p>\n<h2>Baiting</h2>\n<p>Leaving something tempting for the victim to pick up. Classic example: USB drives labelled “Salary Details 2026” dropped in an office parking lot. Curious employees plug them in, and malware installs automatically. Online baiting includes free movie downloads, cracked software, and “free” premium apps.</p>\n<h2>Quid Pro Quo</h2>\n<p>“Something for something.” The attacker offers a service or benefit in exchange for information. Example: “We’re offering free Microsoft 365 upgrades. Just share your login to activate.”</p>\n<h2>Tailgating / Piggybacking</h2>\n<p>A physical technique: someone without an access card follows an employee through a secure door, often carrying boxes so the employee politely holds the door open.</p>\n<h2>Shoulder Surfing and Dumpster Diving</h2>\n<p>Watching someone type a PIN or password, or searching through thrown-away papers for account details, printed emails, or org charts that help plan a phishing attack.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout example\"><h4>Real-Life Example: “Digital Arrest” Scams in India (2024 onwards)</h4>\n<p>One of the most dangerous social engineering scams in India. Victims receive a call saying a parcel in their name contains drugs or fake passports. The call is “transferred” to fake CBI, police, or customs officers, often on a video call with uniforms, badges, and a fake police station background.</p>\n<p>The victim is told they are under “digital arrest”, must stay on camera, must not tell anyone, and must transfer money to a “verification account” to clear their name. Victims have lost amounts from a few thousand to several crores. The scam became so widespread that the Prime Minister warned citizens about it in his Mann Ki Baat address in October 2024.</p>\n<p><strong>Triggers used:</strong> authority (police, CBI), fear (arrest), urgency, and isolation (“don’t tell anyone”).</p>\n<p><strong>Fact:</strong> There is no such thing as “digital arrest” in Indian law. Police never demand money over a video call.</p></aside>\n<h2>5.4 The Isolation Trick</h2>\n<p>Notice how many scams include a line like “Keep this confidential”, “Don’t tell your family”, or “This is a secret project, don’t discuss with the team.” This is deliberate. The attacker knows that the moment you talk to someone else, the spell breaks.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\"><h4>Remember: The Secrecy Red Flag</h4>\n<p>Any request that asks you to keep it secret from your family, colleagues, or manager is a major red flag. Legitimate processes never depend on secrecy from the people around you.</p></aside>",
