   "n": 8,
   "title": "Common Phishing Attacks",
   "time": "45 minutes",
   "html": "<p>In Module 3 we learned the <strong>types</strong> of phishing. In this module, we look at the actual <strong>scams</strong> you are most likely to encounter, especially in India. For each one: how it looks, the red flag, and the right response.</p>\n<h2>8.1 Scams Targeting Everyone</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Scam</strong></th>\n<th><strong>How It Looks</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Right Response</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>KYC / Account Block</strong></td>\n<td>“Your bank account/SIM will be blocked today. Update KYC: [link]”</td>\n<td>Banks don’t block accounts via SMS links. Check in your bank app or branch.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Electricity Bill</strong></td>\n<td>“Power will be disconnected tonight. Call officer at 98XXXXXXXX.”</td>\n<td>Check your bill on the official discom website/app. Never install apps on a caller’s instruction.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Parcel / Courier</strong></td>\n<td>“Your parcel is on hold. Pay ₹25 re-delivery fee: [link]”</td>\n<td>Track only on the official courier website using your real tracking ID.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Drugs Parcel / Digital Arrest</strong></td>\n<td>“A parcel in your name has drugs. Connecting you to CBI.”</td>\n<td>Hang up. There is no “digital arrest”. Report on 1930.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Income Tax Refund</strong></td>\n<td>“Your refund of ₹15,490 is approved. Verify account: [link]”</td>\n<td>Refunds come automatically. Check only on incometax.gov.in.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>UPI “Receive Money”</strong></td>\n<td>“I’ve sent you a request to receive ₹5,000. Just enter your PIN.”</td>\n<td>Entering your UPI PIN always sends money. Never to receive.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fake Customer Care</strong></td>\n<td>You search for a helpline and call a number from a random site or ad.</td>\n<td>Use only numbers from the official website or app.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lottery / KBC Prize</strong></td>\n<td>“You won ₹25 lakh! Pay ₹12,500 processing fee.”</td>\n<td>You can’t win a lottery you never entered. Block and report.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Relative in Trouble</strong></td>\n<td>WhatsApp from new number with relative’s photo: “Phone lost, urgent money needed.”</td>\n<td>Call the relative on their known number. Use your family code word.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sextortion</strong></td>\n<td>Threats to leak intimate photos/videos (often fake or from a trap video call).</td>\n<td>Don’t pay. Preserve evidence, report on cybercrime.gov.in or 1930.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>8.2 Scams Targeting Students</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Scam</strong></th>\n<th><strong>How It Looks</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Right Response</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fake Internship / Job Offer</strong></td>\n<td>Offer letter from a “top company” after no interview. Asks for “registration” or “training kit” fee.</td>\n<td>Genuine companies never charge fees for jobs. Verify on the company’s official careers page.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Task / Like-and-Earn Scam</strong></td>\n<td>“Earn ₹5,000 daily by liking YouTube videos.” Small payouts first, then “invest” to unlock bigger tasks.</td>\n<td>If you have to pay to earn, it’s a scam. Exit immediately.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scholarship Scam</strong></td>\n<td>“You’re selected for a government scholarship. Pay processing fee.”</td>\n<td>Check only on the National Scholarship Portal or official university notices.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fake College / Exam Notice</strong></td>\n<td>Fake portal links for results, fee payment, or admit cards.</td>\n<td>Use only the official college website or ERP you normally use.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Crypto / Trading Groups</strong></td>\n<td>Telegram/WhatsApp groups showing “guaranteed” returns and fake screenshots.</td>\n<td>Guaranteed returns don’t exist. Don’t install apps shared in such groups.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>8.3 Scams Targeting Employees and Organisations</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Scam</strong></th>\n<th><strong>How It Looks</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Right Response</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CEO / Boss Fraud</strong></td>\n<td>“I’m in a meeting. Buy 10 Amazon gift cards for a client and send codes. Keep this quiet.”</td>\n<td>Verify by calling your boss on a known number. Gift card requests are almost always scams.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vendor Bank Change (BEC)</strong></td>\n<td>“We’ve changed our bank. Please use this new account for the pending invoice.”</td>\n<td>Call the vendor on a number from your records before changing any bank details.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fake Invoice</strong></td>\n<td>Invoice for services never received, often with an urgent due date.</td>\n<td>Match with purchase orders. Confirm with the requesting department.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Password Expiry / Microsoft 365</strong></td>\n<td>“Your password expires today. Keep the same password: [link]”</td>\n<td>Change passwords only through your usual company portal. Report to IT.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shared Document</strong></td>\n<td>“Rahul shared ‘Q3_Budget.xlsx’ with you” linking to a fake login page.</td>\n<td>Open shared files directly from your Drive/OneDrive, not the email link.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>MFA Fatigue</strong></td>\n<td>Repeated MFA approval pop-ups you didn’t trigger.</td>\n<td>Never approve. Deny and report immediately — your password is already stolen.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fake IT Support Call</strong></td>\n<td>“This is IT. We’re fixing an issue. Please install this tool / read me the code.”</td>\n<td>Hang up and call your IT helpdesk on the official number.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HR / Payroll</strong></td>\n<td>Appraisal letters, policy updates, or payroll portal links demanding login.</td>\n<td>Access HR systems only via the usual bookmarked link.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout example\"><h4>Real-Life Example: The Gift Card Trick</h4>\n<p>This is one of the most common scams against employees worldwide. An attacker creates a WhatsApp account with the CEO’s photo and messages a junior employee: “Are you at your desk? I need a quick favour, I’m in a board meeting.” It escalates to buying gift cards “for a client” and sending the codes. Gift cards are the attacker’s favourite because they are instant and nearly impossible to trace.</p></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout tip\"><h4>Quick Tip: The Universal Response</h4>\n<p>For any of the scams above, the response is the same three steps: <strong>Stop</strong> (don’t click, pay, or share), <strong>Verify</strong> (through a trusted channel), <strong>Report</strong> (to IT, your bank, or 1930).</p></aside>",
