   "n": 9,
   "title": "Precautions to Stay Safe",
   "time": "50 minutes",
   "html": "<p>Knowing how to spot phishing is half the job. The other half is building habits and defences so that even if you make a mistake, the damage is limited.</p>\n<h2>9.1 Personal Precautions</h2>\n<h2>1. Turn on Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)</h2>\n<p>MFA means that even if someone steals your password, they still need a second proof — a code from your phone, an app approval, or a security key. Turn it on for email, banking, social media, and cloud storage.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>MFA Method</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Security Level</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SMS OTP</td>\n<td>⭐⭐ Better than nothing, but can be phished or intercepted via SIM swap.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Authenticator app (Google/Microsoft Authenticator)</td>\n<td>⭐⭐⭐ Good. Codes stay on your device.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Push approval with number matching</td>\n<td>⭐⭐⭐ Good, and resists MFA fatigue.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Passkeys / hardware security keys (FIDO2)</td>\n<td>⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Best. Phishing-resistant because they only work on the real website.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>2. Use a password manager</h2>\n<p>Use a unique, strong password for every account. A password manager (like Bitwarden, 1Password, or your browser’s built-in manager) remembers them for you. Bonus: it won’t auto-fill your password on a fake website, because the domain doesn’t match. <strong>That’s a built-in phishing alarm.</strong></p>\n<h2>3. Never share OTP, PIN, CVV, or passwords</h2>\n<p>With anyone. Ever. Not with bank staff, police, customer care, IT support, or family members on a call.</p>\n<h2>4. Go direct, don’t follow links</h2>\n<p>For banking, government services, shopping, and college portals, type the address yourself, use a bookmark, or use the official app. Don’t log in through links in messages.</p>\n<h2>5. Keep devices updated</h2>\n<p>Install updates for your phone, laptop, browser, and apps. Many attacks exploit old, unpatched software.</p>\n<h2>6. Install apps only from official stores</h2>\n<p>Never install APK files sent on WhatsApp or SMS, and never install remote access apps (AnyDesk, TeamViewer, QuickSupport) on a stranger’s instruction.</p>\n<h2>7. Reduce your public footprint</h2>\n<p>Review your privacy settings on Instagram, Facebook, and LinkedIn. Avoid posting your phone number, date of birth, college ID, boarding passes, or offer letters publicly. Everything you share is research material for spear phishing.</p>\n<h2>8. Protect your UPI and bank accounts</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Set daily transaction limits in your banking app.</p></li>\n<li><p>Enable SMS and email alerts for every transaction.</p></li>\n<li><p>Keep a separate account with limited balance for online payments, if possible.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout tip\"><h4>Quick Tip: The 10-Second Pause</h4>\n<p>Before clicking any link or making any payment triggered by a message, count to ten and ask: “Did I start this conversation?” If you didn’t, verify first.</p></aside>\n<h2>9.2 Organisational Defences</h2>\n<p>This section is especially for IT and security teams, but every employee benefits from knowing what protects them.</p>\n<h2>Email Authentication: SPF, DKIM, DMARC</h2>\n<p>Think of these as the anti-forgery features of your company’s letterhead:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Protocol</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Simple Explanation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SPF</strong> (Sender Policy Framework)</td>\n<td>A public list of servers allowed to send email for your domain. Like a guest list at the door.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DKIM</strong> (DomainKeys Identified Mail)</td>\n<td>A digital signature on each email proving it wasn’t altered and came from your domain. Like a wax seal on a letter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DMARC</strong></td>\n<td>A policy that tells receiving servers what to do if SPF/DKIM fail: nothing, send to spam, or reject. It also sends reports. Organisations should aim for a “p=reject” policy.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>Other key controls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Email filtering and sandboxing:</strong> scans links and attachments before they reach inboxes.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>External email banner:</strong> a warning tag like “[EXTERNAL] This email came from outside the organisation.” Very effective against CEO impersonation.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Phishing-resistant MFA:</strong> move critical accounts (admins, finance, leadership) to passkeys or hardware keys.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Least privilege:</strong> give employees only the access they need, so one compromised account can’t reach everything.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Payment verification process:</strong> any new vendor, bank change, or urgent payment must be confirmed by a call-back to a known number, with two-person approval above a limit.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Report Phishing button:</strong> a one-click way for employees to report suspicious emails to the security team.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Lookalike domain monitoring:</strong> detect and take down domains that imitate your brand.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Regular phishing simulations and training:</strong> safe, realistic test emails followed by short learning moments — not punishment.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\"><h4>Remember: Blame Culture Helps Attackers</h4>\n<p>If employees fear punishment for clicking a phishing link, they will hide it — and the attacker gets hours or days of free access. A good security culture rewards fast reporting, even after a mistake.</p></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout try\"><h4>Try It Yourself: Your Personal Security Checklist</h4>\n<p>Today: (1) Turn on MFA for your primary email. (2) Check your email on haveibeenpwned.com. (3) Set a daily UPI limit. (4) Agree on a family code word. (5) Bookmark your bank and college portal login pages.</p></aside>",
