   "n": 10,
   "title": "Incident Response: “I Clicked. Now What?”",
   "time": "40 minutes",
   "html": "<p>Everyone makes mistakes. What separates a small incident from a disaster is <strong>how fast you act after the mistake</strong>. This module is your emergency guide. Keep it handy.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\"><h4>Remember: The Golden Hour</h4>\n<p>If you have lost money, report it within the first few hours. The faster a fraud is reported to 1930, the higher the chance that the money can be frozen before the criminal withdraws it.</p></aside>\n<h2>10.1 What To Do, Based on What Happened</h2>\n<h2>Scenario A: I only clicked the link (didn’t enter anything)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Close the page immediately. Don’t download or enter anything.</p></li>\n<li><p>Run an antivirus scan on your device.</p></li>\n<li><p>If it was a work device, inform your IT/security team — they may want to block the site for everyone.</p></li>\n<li><p>Watch for unusual activity over the next few days.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Scenario B: I entered my password</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Change the password immediately</strong> from the official website or app (not the link you clicked).</p></li>\n<li><p>If you reused that password elsewhere, change it on those accounts too.</p></li>\n<li><p>Turn on MFA if it wasn’t already on.</p></li>\n<li><p>Check for unknown logins, forwarding rules, or recovery email/phone changes in account settings.</p></li>\n<li><p>Log out of all other sessions.</p></li>\n<li><p>For work accounts: report to IT <strong>immediately</strong>, even before changing the password if your company’s process requires it.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Scenario C: I shared bank details, OTP, card number, or lost money</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Call your bank’s official helpline</strong> and block your card, UPI, and net banking.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Call 1930</strong> (National Cyber Crime Helpline) to report the financial fraud.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>File a complaint on cybercrime.gov.in</strong> with all details.</p></li>\n<li><p>Note the complaint/acknowledgement number for future follow-up.</p></li>\n<li><p>Visit your bank branch or local cyber police station if asked.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Scenario D: I installed an app or opened an attachment</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Disconnect from the internet</strong> (turn off Wi-Fi and mobile data).</p></li>\n<li><p>Uninstall any unknown or remote access app (AnyDesk, TeamViewer, etc.).</p></li>\n<li><p>Run a full antivirus scan. On a work device, don’t try to fix it yourself — hand it to IT.</p></li>\n<li><p>From a different, clean device, change passwords for email and banking.</p></li>\n<li><p>If the phone was used for banking, contact your bank to block and reissue credentials.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Scenario E: My social media or WhatsApp account was hacked</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Use the platform’s official account recovery process.</p></li>\n<li><p>Warn your contacts through another channel that messages from your account may be fake.</p></li>\n<li><p>For WhatsApp, re-register your number on your phone and set up two-step verification (a 6-digit PIN).</p></li>\n<li><p>Report the account compromise to the platform.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>10.2 Preserve the Evidence</h2>\n<p>Evidence helps police, banks, and IT teams. Before deleting anything:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Take screenshots of messages, emails, websites, payment screens, and caller numbers.</p></li>\n<li><p>Note transaction IDs (UTR numbers), dates, times, and amounts.</p></li>\n<li><p>Keep the original email (don’t just forward a screenshot — security teams need the headers).</p></li>\n<li><p>Don’t argue with or threaten the scammer; just stop contact.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>10.3 Where to Report in India</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Where</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What For</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1930</strong> (National Cyber Crime Helpline)</td>\n<td>Immediate reporting of financial fraud.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>cybercrime.gov.in</strong></td>\n<td>Online complaint for all cybercrimes, including financial fraud and women/child-related crimes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sanchar Saathi – Chakshu</strong> (sancharsaathi.gov.in)</td>\n<td>Reporting suspected fraud calls, SMS, and WhatsApp messages, even if you didn’t lose money.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Your bank’s official helpline</strong></td>\n<td>Blocking cards, accounts, and disputing transactions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CERT-In</strong> (incident@cert-in.org.in)</td>\n<td>Cyber security incidents, especially for organisations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Your IT / Security team</strong></td>\n<td>Anything involving a work account, device, or data.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>112</strong></td>\n<td>Emergency police response, e.g. if you are being threatened.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>10.4 For Organisations: The Response Process</h2>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>Report:</strong> employee reports via the Report Phishing button or helpdesk.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Triage:</strong> security team checks the email, links, and attachments.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Contain:</strong> reset passwords, revoke sessions, block the sender domain and URL, remove the email from all mailboxes, isolate infected devices.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Investigate:</strong> find out who else received or clicked, and what data was accessed.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Notify:</strong> inform management, legal, and regulators where required. Under CERT-In’s 2022 directions, certain cyber incidents, including phishing attacks, must be reported to CERT-In within 6 hours of being noticed.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>Learn:</strong> update filters, training, and processes so it doesn’t happen again.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<aside class=\"callout tip\"><h4>Quick Tip: Report Even If You’re Not Sure</h4>\n<p>You don’t need to be certain it’s phishing to report it. Security teams would much rather check 100 false alarms than miss one real attack.</p></aside>",
