   "n": 11,
   "title": "Legal Framework (India)",
   "time": "30 minutes",
   "html": "<p>Phishing is not just unethical — it is a crime. And organisations also have legal duties to protect the data they hold. This module gives you a simple overview. It is for awareness only and is not legal advice.</p>\n<h2>11.1 The Information Technology Act, 2000</h2>\n<p>The IT Act is India’s primary law for cybercrime. Key sections related to phishing:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Section</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What It Covers</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Punishment (Summary)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Section 43</strong></td>\n<td>Unauthorised access to a computer or data, downloading data, introducing viruses.</td>\n<td>Compensation to the affected person.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Section 66</strong></td>\n<td>Doing any act under Section 43 dishonestly or fraudulently.</td>\n<td>Up to 3 years imprisonment and/or fine up to ₹5 lakh.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Section 66C</strong></td>\n<td>Identity theft: fraudulently using someone’s password, electronic signature, or other unique identification.</td>\n<td>Up to 3 years imprisonment and fine up to ₹1 lakh.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Section 66D</strong></td>\n<td>Cheating by personation using a computer resource — the core phishing offence.</td>\n<td>Up to 3 years imprisonment and fine up to ₹1 lakh.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Section 70B</strong></td>\n<td>Establishes CERT-In as the national agency for cyber incident response.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>11.2 Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita (BNS), 2023</h2>\n<p>From 1 July 2024, the BNS replaced the Indian Penal Code (IPC). Phishing cases are often also charged under BNS provisions for cheating (the old IPC Section 420 is now covered under BNS Section 318), forgery, and extortion, alongside the IT Act.</p>\n<h2>11.3 Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) Act, 2023</h2>\n<p>The DPDP Act is India’s data protection law. It matters for phishing because many breaches start with a phishing email.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>Data Fiduciaries</strong> (organisations that decide how personal data is used) must take reasonable security safeguards to prevent personal data breaches.</p></li>\n<li><p>If a breach happens, the organisation must inform the <strong>Data Protection Board of India</strong> and the affected individuals.</p></li>\n<li><p>Failure to take reasonable security safeguards can attract penalties of up to <strong>₹250 crore</strong>.</p></li>\n<li><p>The DPDP Rules, 2025 are being implemented in phases, so organisations should prepare now.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What this means for employees:</strong> when you fall for phishing at work, it’s not just your problem. It can create legal liability for your organisation. That’s why reporting quickly is so important.</p>\n<h2>11.4 CERT-In Directions (April 2022)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Organisations must report specified cyber incidents, including phishing attacks, to CERT-In within <strong>6 hours</strong> of noticing them.</p></li>\n<li><p>Organisations must maintain logs of their ICT systems for a rolling period of 180 days within India.</p></li>\n<li><p>They must also designate a point of contact to coordinate with CERT-In.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>11.5 Your Rights as a Victim</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>You can file a cybercrime complaint online at cybercrime.gov.in or at any police station. Police cannot refuse to register a complaint citing jurisdiction — a “Zero FIR” can be filed at any station.</p></li>\n<li><p>Under RBI guidelines, if you report an unauthorised electronic banking transaction quickly (within 3 working days) and weren’t at fault, your liability can be zero. Delays increase your liability.</p></li>\n<li><p>You can approach the Banking Ombudsman if your bank doesn’t resolve your complaint.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout warn\"><h4>Remember: Don’t Be an Accidental Criminal</h4>\n<p>Some scams recruit students as “money mules”: “Let us use your bank account to receive payments and keep 10% commission.” This is a crime. Your account will be frozen and you can be charged for helping launder fraud money. Never rent or lend your bank account, UPI ID, or SIM card.</p></aside>",
