   "n": 12,
   "title": "Acceptable Use Policy (AUP)",
   "time": "35 minutes",
   "html": "<h2>12.1 What is an AUP?</h2>\n<p>An <strong>Acceptable Use Policy (AUP)</strong> is a set of rules that tells employees (or students) how they can and cannot use the organisation’s technology: computers, email, internet, Wi-Fi, software, and data. When you join a company or college, you usually agree to it — often by clicking “I accept” without reading it.</p>\n<p>Think of it as the “rules of the house”. You’re a guest using someone else’s systems, and the AUP explains what keeps everyone safe.</p>\n<h2>12.2 Why the AUP Matters for Phishing</h2>\n<p>Many AUP rules exist specifically to reduce phishing risk. Following them isn’t bureaucracy — it’s protection. Violating them can also make you personally responsible if an incident happens.</p>\n<h2>12.3 Typical AUP Clauses Related to Phishing</h2>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<colgroup>\n<col />\n<col />\n<col />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>AUP Area</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What It Usually Says</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Why It Matters</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Credentials</strong></td>\n<td>Never share your password, OTP, or access card. You are responsible for all activity on your account.</td>\n<td>Stolen or shared credentials are the main goal of phishing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Email Use</strong></td>\n<td>Use official email only for work. Don’t forward company data to personal email.</td>\n<td>Personal email lacks company security filters and monitoring.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Links &amp; Attachments</strong></td>\n<td>Don’t open unexpected attachments or click suspicious links. Report them.</td>\n<td>Direct defence against phishing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Software Installation</strong></td>\n<td>Install only approved software. No cracked, pirated, or unknown apps.</td>\n<td>Pirated software is a common malware source.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reporting Duty</strong></td>\n<td>Report suspected phishing and security incidents immediately.</td>\n<td>Fast reporting limits damage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Personal Devices (BYOD)</strong></td>\n<td>Personal devices accessing company data must follow security rules (screen lock, updates, approved apps).</td>\n<td>Unsecured phones are easy targets.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Data Handling</strong></td>\n<td>Classify and share data only with authorised people through approved channels.</td>\n<td>Prevents data leaks via fake requests.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Public Wi-Fi &amp; Remote Work</strong></td>\n<td>Use VPN on public networks. Lock screens when away.</td>\n<td>Reduces interception and shoulder surfing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Social Media</strong></td>\n<td>Don’t share internal information, org charts, or photos of badges and screens.</td>\n<td>Limits reconnaissance for spear phishing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monitoring</strong></td>\n<td>The organisation may monitor systems for security purposes.</td>\n<td>Helps detect attacks early.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Consequences</strong></td>\n<td>Violations can lead to disciplinary action, termination, or legal action.</td>\n<td>Makes responsibilities clear.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2>12.4 Sample AUP Statements (Template)</h2>\n<p>Organisations can adapt these statements for their own AUP:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>“Users must not disclose their passwords, OTPs, or authentication codes to anyone, including IT staff.”</p></li>\n<li><p>“Users must report any suspected phishing email, message, or call to the IT Security team within one hour using the Report Phishing button or helpdesk.”</p></li>\n<li><p>“Users must not approve any multi-factor authentication request they did not initiate.”</p></li>\n<li><p>“Any request to change vendor bank details or make an urgent payment must be verified by phone using contact details from official records.”</p></li>\n<li><p>“Users who report a security mistake promptly will not face disciplinary action for the mistake itself.”</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>12.5 For Students: College IT Policies</h2>\n<p>Colleges have similar policies for campus Wi-Fi, college email IDs, ERP portals, and computer labs. Common rules: don’t share your ERP login, don’t use college email for unrelated sign-ups, and report suspicious emails claiming to be from the administration or exam cell.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout try\"><h4>Try It Yourself: Read Your AUP</h4>\n<p>Find your organisation’s or college’s IT/Acceptable Use Policy (usually on the intranet, HR portal, or student handbook). Identify: (1) how to report phishing, (2) the rule on password sharing, (3) the consequence of violations.</p></aside>",
